### Layer 4 — Server-Side Facebook CAPI

#### [NEW] Server Route in Express API
- `POST /api/v1/track/purchase` — accepts `{ orderId, event_id, fbp, fbc, client_ip, client_user_agent }`
- Fetches order from DB (grand_total, items, customer email/phone)
- SHA-256 hashes PII before sending to Meta
- Calls Meta Conversions API with `event_name: "Purchase"`, same `event_id` as browser

#### [NEW] Server Route in Express API  
- `POST /api/v1/track/registration` — accepts `{ event_id, fbp, fbc, client_ip, client_user_agent }`
- Fires [CompleteRegistration](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_shop/lib/analytics/facebook-pixel.ts#228-232) to Meta CAPI

#### [NEW] `helpers/capi.js` in API
- `sendCapiEvent(connection, eventName, eventData, userData, serverData)` — wraps Meta Graph API call
- SHA-256 hash utility for email + phone (normalised: lowercase, strip spaces/dashes)
- Reads FB Pixel ID + CAPI Access Token from `system_config` table
- Fires asynchronously (does NOT block API response)
- Logs result to console (no DB write needed)

#### [MODIFY] [fallback.js](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_api/controllers/fallback.js) or [order.js](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_api/controllers/order.js) in API
- In the [processSuccessfulPayment()](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_api/helpers/payment.js#143-201) function in [helpers/payment.js](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_api/helpers/payment.js) — after DB commit, call `sendCapiEvent()` for [Purchase](file:///d:/gcp/gcp_graduatefashion_shop/lib/analytics/facebook-pixel.ts#208-219) asynchronously

